---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/postscript-vs-sendlane';
const title = 'Postscript vs Sendlane for Shopify: 2026 Comparison';
const desc = 'Postscript vs Sendlane for Shopify stores — email and SMS ownership after the Privy acquisition, contract scope, migration rehearsals, and stack costs.';

const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is Postscript or Sendlane better for Shopify SMS?",
    "answer": "Choose against the work your team needs to own. Postscript now advertises Email as well as SMS; Sendlane has joined Privy. Compare the products actually offered to your account, the existing journeys you would retain, and the handoffs you would introduce. Neither channel revenue share nor list size establishes a winner."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can Sendlane replace Postscript entirely?",
    "answer": "It is a candidate only if the offered account covers your sending destinations, number migration, automations, replies and support requirements. Verify those requirements with a demonstration and written scope. The acquisition announcement alone does not establish a replacement package for an existing Postscript account."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should I use Sendlane for email and Postscript for SMS?",
    "answer": "That split can preserve working Sendlane email journeys while keeping SMS with its current owner. Define one sender per message purpose, purchase cancellation across both systems, and who handles replies. Compare this ongoing coordination with the work of consolidating both channels."
  },
  {
    "question": "How does Postscript vs Sendlane pricing compare?",
    "answer": "Use written quotes for the same email sends, SMS message mix, destinations and support scope. Sendlane advertises send-based email pricing and separate SMS packages; dedicated support is tied to annual contracts on its pricing page. Include overlapping subscriptions, migration work and renewal commitments rather than assuming SMS is free."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should I also consider Sequenzy instead of Sendlane or Postscript?",
    "answer": "Sequenzy is another email automation candidate if rebuilding lifecycle messaging is part of the project. Its pricing page advertises email-send billing and an SMS/MMS beta add-on. Check Shopify events, migration assistance and the beta scope separately; it is not automatically an equivalent replacement for either existing stack."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which has better email automation for Shopify?",
    "answer": "Postscript has announced Email, so an SMS-only comparison is outdated. Sendlane advertises email automation and Shopify integration. Compare your actual templates, exclusions, event branches and sending-domain setup in each offered account instead of declaring a general email winner."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which has stronger TCPA compliance for SMS?",
    "answer": "This comparison does not establish a compliance winner. Have the responsible operator review the intended signup, suppression and sending configuration for each destination. Rehearse an opt-out while messages are queued and verify the result in every connected sender; software marketing is not a legal assessment."
  },
  {
    "question": "What workflow test should decide the winner?",
    "answer": "Run a proposed subscription restock rehearsal: email education, an eligible SMS reminder, a reply requesting a change, a purchase before the queued reminder, and a delayed duplicate event. Record which system owns each step and whether cancellation and handoff work. These are acceptance tests, not reported merchant results."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-cyan-100/90">/</span>
        <span class="text-cyan-100/90">Postscript vs Sendlane</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="badge">Head-to-head</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Postscript vs Sendlane for Shopify</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-cyan-100">For a Shopify team already using Sendlane email and Postscript SMS, the decision is whether to preserve that split or rebuild both channels with one owner. Privy's acquisition of Sendlane and Postscript's Email launch change the shortlist. Start with the account and contract you can actually obtain, then rehearse the migration before canceling a working sender.</p>
        <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-8 text-cyan-100/90">The subscription restock example below is a proposed evaluation, not a merchant case study. It focuses on queued reminders, subscription changes and responsibility during a cutover.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox
          picks={[
            { label: 'Preserve a split', tool: 'Sendlane + Postscript', reason: 'Keep working journeys when coordination costs less than rebuilding them.' },
            { label: 'Consolidate channels', tool: 'Evaluate both offers', reason: 'Compare account access, migration scope and support commitments.' },
            { label: 'Email rebuild candidate', tool: 'Sequenzy', reason: 'Assess email automation and the separate SMS/MMS beta scope.' },
          ]}
        />
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto">
        <h2 class="px-5 py-4 font-serif text-2xl font-black text-white">Comparison at a glance</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Postscript</th><th>Sendlane</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Current context</td><td>SMS plus announced Email offering</td><td>Acquired by Privy</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Email migration</td><td>Launch materials advertise migration tooling; confirm access</td><td>Pricing page advertises migration assistance; confirm tier</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Existing split</td><td>Can remain the SMS owner in the proposed architecture</td><td>Can remain the email owner in the proposed architecture</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Commercial scope</td><td>Obtain a quote for the offered channel mix</td><td>Send-based email pricing, SMS packages and contract-dependent support</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Acceptance evidence</td><td>Demonstrate imported flows and SMS handoff</td><td>Demonstrate retained journeys and transition responsibilities</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Separate the acquisition from your contract</h2>
        <p><a class="link" href="https://www.privy.com/blog/privy-acquires-sendlane">Privy's acquisition announcement</a> confirms that Sendlane joined Privy. That establishes ownership, not the renewal terms, migration deadline or feature entitlement for your account. Keep the current order form beside the replacement proposal and identify the contracting party, renewal date, support owner, export access and any overlap in paid service.</p>
        <p>The <a class="link" href="https://www.sendlane.com/pricing">Sendlane pricing page</a> advertises email-send billing, SMS packages and migration services. It also ties dedicated support to annual contracts. Ask which services are included in your signed tier, who rebuilds branches and exclusions, and who fixes discrepancies after cutover. A public migration benefit is not proof that every legacy contract includes the same work.</p>

        <h2>Postscript Email adds a consolidation option</h2>
        <p>Postscript's <a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/launch">launch page</a> announces Email and describes profile identity resolution, attribution options and migration tooling. The <a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/email">Email page</a> invites interested teams to get on the list. Treat access and onboarding scope as account-specific questions; an announcement is not proof that your store can move every flow immediately.</p>
        <p>Compare three concrete plans: retain Sendlane email with Postscript SMS, obtain a Sendlane/Privy offer covering both channels, or evaluate Postscript for both. The split avoids some email rebuilding but leaves cross-system coordination. Consolidation reduces the number of operators and integrations only if the destination reproduces the behavior you need.</p>

        <h2>Proposed rehearsal: subscription restock with a change request</h2>
        <p>Use a test subscription product that can be bought once or added to a recurring order. Email explains the restock and subscription terms; SMS supplies a time-sensitive reminder only to the eligible audience. Decide which system knows subscription status and whether that data comes from Shopify or a separate subscription integration. A normal order event does not by itself demonstrate that the recurring order was updated.</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Create separate test profiles for an email subscriber, an SMS subscriber and someone eligible for both. Check that each receives only the intended channel sequence.</li>
          <li>Have the dual-channel profile reply asking to add the product to next month's box. Route that request to a named operator with access to the subscription system. Record the resolution before any follow-up promise is sent.</li>
          <li>Complete a purchase before the queued reminder. Inspect both systems to confirm the reminder is canceled and a later email does not offer an already-completed purchase as unfinished.</li>
          <li>Replay a delayed order event and a duplicate event. Confirm neither creates a second enrollment or reverses the resolved state. Test an opt-out while a message is queued, then inspect the destination sender's suppression state.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Keep timestamps and expected outcomes for each step. A failed handoff identifies a specific integration or staffing gap; it does not establish a general performance ranking. No live store results or throughput benchmarks are claimed here.</p>

        <h2>Give every transition a single owner</h2>
        <p>Before changing tools, inventory signup forms, welcome flows, cart recovery, replenishment and reply queues. For each, record the current sender, replacement sender, event source and disable time. Leave the old configuration recoverable while preventing it from sending alongside its replacement.</p>
        <p>For a split stack, nominate an owner for cross-channel cancellation and resolve conflicting segment definitions before a restock. For consolidation, test whether historical exclusions, consent records and subscription identifiers survive import. Bringing over a template does not establish that its dynamic content, discount rules and audience exclusions survived too.</p>

        <h2>Compare costs over the transition and renewal</h2>
        <p>Request quotes using the same email-send forecast, SMS destinations, message segmentation, replies and peak calendar. Include recurring fees, usage charges, support commitments, import work and the period when both tools remain paid. Keep claimed attribution separate from incremental contribution; two dashboards can claim the same order.</p>
        <p>Hypothetical transition budget: two months of overlap at $250 per month plus 12 hours of rebuild work at $50 per hour costs $1,100. If verified ongoing savings were $100 per month, recovering that transition cost would take 11 months. These are illustrative inputs, not supplier prices or observed savings. Lost contribution or extra support work would lengthen that period.</p>

        <h2>Where Sequenzy fits</h2>
        <p><a class="link" href="https://www.sequenzy.com/pricing">Sequenzy's pricing page</a> advertises email automation billed by email volume and an SMS/MMS beta add-on. Include it when the email rebuild itself needs a fresh candidate. Assess event mapping, exclusions and the subscription-change handoff in the same rehearsal; do not treat a beta add-on as demonstrated parity with the existing SMS operation.</p>

        <h2>Make the decision from the work you can retire</h2>
        <p>Keep Sendlane email and Postscript SMS when preserving working journeys is worth the coordination effort and the rehearsal passes. Consolidate when the offered account covers the required channels, the contract assigns migration responsibility, and the rebuilt journeys pass acceptance. Delay cancellation when number transfer, exports or support ownership remain unresolved. Neither a promised saving nor a channel revenue threshold substitutes for those checks.</p>
      </article>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-white">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/sendlane-vs-klaviyo">Sendlane vs Klaviyo</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/rejoiner-vs-sendlane">Rejoiner vs Sendlane</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-postscript">Klaviyo vs Postscript</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/supplements">Marketing for supplement stores</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Postscript vs Sendlane FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
